<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Mahathat, Sukhothai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Wat Mahathat (Sukhothai)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Mahathat is the principal temple at the centre of the old city of Sukhothai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, the kingdom's royal and religious centre. Its main chedi, crowned by a lotus-bud spire characteristic of Sukhothai architecture, is surrounded by subsidiary stupas, assembly halls and large Buddha images. It is part of the Sukhothai Historical Park, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-mahathat-sukhothai.jpg" alt="Wat Mahathat, Sukhothai" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khunkay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_srichum_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rulers adopted the Theravada Buddhism of Sri Lanka, brought by monks via Nakhon Si Thammarat in the south, and built monasteries such as <b>Wat Mahathat</b> at the centre of Sukhothai and Wat Si Chum with its giant seated Buddha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
